From a67a219509b253dfe06383b112ceab2fa7d41c6c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bjorn Pagen <11238136+bjornpagen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 16:58:21 -0600
Subject: [PATCH REL_19 v1] Choose subplan names in constant time

choose_plan_name(), added by 8c49a484e8e, checks a name against those
already assigned by scanning the list glob->subplanNames, and finds a
numeric suffix by trying 1, 2, 3, ... with a scan of the list for each.
So the k-th subplan of a kind (exists_k, expr_k, setop_k,
unnamed_subquery_k and so on) costs about k scans of a list of every
name assigned so far, and a statement with N of them O(N^3) string
comparisons.  A SELECT of 4000 uncorrelated EXISTS sublinks takes 47.5 s
of backend CPU time to plan, and in the statements an application of
ours generates, with a few hundred sublinks, unnamed subqueries and
CTEs each, choose_plan_name() takes 40-65% of planning time.

Keep the names in a hash table instead, keyed by the C string, so that
names of any length work.  Each entry records whether the name itself
has been assigned and, for a name that has been the prefix of a
numbered name, the suffix to try next: every smaller suffix was found
to be in use, and names are never given up, so none of them can have
become free.  Choosing a name then takes constant time on average, and
the 4000 EXISTS sublinks plan in 0.43 s.  The table is created on the
first call, in the current memory context, as the list was.

The names chosen are the same as before: the name as given if it is not
in use and numbering is not required, else the name with the smallest
numeric suffix not in use.  The new regression test, which shows
numbered names next to CTE names and FROM-clause aliases at several
query levels and 200 sublinks of two kinds, gives the same output
without this change.

This is the design Robert Haas proposed in the discussion, without a
threshold below which the list is kept.

In this branch, glob->subplanNames stays a list, maintained as before
for any extension that reads it, and the hash table is a new field,
subplanNamesHash, at the end of PlannerGlobal, so that no existing
field moves: extensions reach PlannerGlobal through hooks, and
GetPlannerGlobalExtensionState() reads its extension_state fields
inline.  PlannerGlobal is allocated only by planner.c, with makeNode().

Reported-by: Manuel Reyes Bravo
Suggested-by: Robert Haas <robertmhaas@gmail.com>
Discussion: https://postgr.es/m/179161574267.1707709.13345376006450096249@gmail.com
Backpatch-through: 19
